WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.340
Three numbers run every purification.

2
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:13.380
Specific activity is enzyme units divided by total protein a measure of how much of

3
00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:17.420
your sample is the target, and it must rise at every good step.

4
00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.920
Fold-purification is the final specific activity divided by the starting one.

5
00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:27.260
Percent yield is how much of your original activity you kept.

6
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:33.280
Picture a beaker of mixed marbles: the same number of red marbles in a smaller pile

7
00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:34.980
is a higher specific activity.

8
00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:39.890
Four physical properties, four separation methods.

9
00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:46.770
Solubility: salting out with ammonium sulfate drops proteins out of solution at

10
00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.190
different salt concentrations.

11
00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:55.730
Size: gel filtration, where counterintuitively the large proteins exit first.

12
00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:01.790
Charge: ion exchange, where opposite charges stick to the beads.

13
00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:08.450
Binding affinity: a bead decorated with something only your protein recognizes.

14
00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:12.830
Match the method to whatever you already know about your target.

15
00:01:13.683 --> 00:01:19.143
Ion exchange separates by charge, and the rule is just opposites attract.

16
00:01:19.843 --> 00:01:25.723
A cation exchanger carries negative beads and binds positive proteins use it when

17
00:01:25.723 --> 00:01:28.543
the buffer pH is below your protein's pI.

18
00:01:29.203 --> 00:01:35.043
An anion exchanger carries positive beads and binds negative proteins use it when pH

19
00:01:35.043 --> 00:01:41.183
is above the pI. Once bound, raise the salt or shift the pH and your protein lets

20
00:01:41.183 --> 00:01:42.183
go.

21
00:01:42.283 --> 00:01:50.323
SDS coats every protein with uniform negative charge, so the gel sorts by size alone

22
00:01:51.283 --> 00:01:53.903
small proteins migrate faster, toward the bottom.

23
00:01:55.043 --> 00:02:01.243
Reading it: fewer bands means a purer sample; a band's position gives molecular

24
00:02:01.243 --> 00:02:07.283
weight, compared to the ladder; band intensity reflects how much protein is there.

25
00:02:07.983 --> 00:02:13.803
The goal of a purification is a single band one protein, finally alone.

26
00:02:14.833 --> 00:02:18.793
Mass spectrometry weighs molecules to identify them.

27
00:02:19.453 --> 00:02:25.553
In MALDI-TOF, a laser blasts your protein off a matrix-coated plate and ionizes

28
00:02:25.553 --> 00:02:31.953
it; the ions fly down an evacuated tube, and the time of flight tells you the mass

29
00:02:32.693 --> 00:02:34.213
heavier ions arrive later.

30
00:02:35.093 --> 00:02:40.633
It needs only picomoles of sample and can resolve proteins above ten kilodaltons.

31
00:02:40.633 --> 00:02:45.553
Weigh a protein's tryptic peptides and you can identify it from a database.
